# Investigation outputs
#
# Ignored as DIRECTORIES, which is what makes them airtight: git does not descend into an
# ignored directory, so nothing underneath one can be resurrected by a negation further down
# this file. The default case directory (`outputs/cases/<scope>-<case id>/<run id>/`) sits under
# `outputs/` for exactly that reason -- its `case.json`, `report.md` and evidence envelopes are
# covered by the directory rule and do not depend on the `*.json` rule below.
#
# `cases/` is listed separately because `--case-dir ./cases` is the obvious thing to type, and a
# case directory holds full provider responses about a live investigation. A `--case-dir` pointed
# anywhere else is the operator's own call and is NOT covered; `write_case` records which of the
# two it was in the case record's `git_ignored_location` field. See docs/OPSEC.md section 5a.
outputs/
results/
reports/
cases/

# OSINT data dumps (prevents pushing JSON or CSV data)
*.json
*.csv
*.tsv
*.sqlite
*.db

# Temporary targets lists
*.txt
!requirements.txt
!LICENSE.txt

# Source data that must stay committable
#
# The blanket *.json / *.csv / *.tsv / *.txt rules above exist to stop
# investigation output from ever reaching the remote. They also swallow two
# kinds of file that are source, not output: recorded provider fixtures under
# tests/, and a future JSON Schema for the report artifact under schema/.
# Re-include those two paths, by extension only.
#
# This does not weaken the output rule. outputs/, results/ and reports/ are
# ignored as *directories* (lines 80-82), and git does not descend into an
# ignored directory, so no negation here can resurrect investigation output.
# The negations are scoped to two named directories and to data extensions, so
# a stray dump anywhere else stays ignored.
!tests/**/*.json
!tests/**/*.csv
!tests/**/*.tsv
!tests/**/*.txt
!schema/**/*.json
